  ('cooking-for-yourself', 364, 'Bake a small batch of biscuits from a mix and eat one warm with butter and honey.'),
  ('cooking-for-yourself', 365, 'Celebrate the end of a long day by cooking your favorite comfort meal and eating it slowly at the table.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 1, 'Write down the three relatives you see most often and one word for how you usually feel after time with each.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 2, 'Notice which family member''s name on your phone makes your shoulders tighten, and take one slow breath before you answer next time.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 3, 'Before calling your dad, choose one easy topic you''d both enjoy, like a ballgame, a recipe or a show.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 4, 'Jot down the role you tend to play at family gatherings, like peacemaker, joker or helper, and ask whether you still want it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 5, 'Pick one phrase to use when a hot-button topic comes up, such as "Let''s leave that one for today."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 6, 'Ask a grandparent or older relative where they lived when they were your age, and what their street looked like.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 7, 'Text a sibling about a happy memory from childhood, like a trip, a silly game or a favorite meal.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 8, 'Let one small comment from a relative pass today without correcting it, and notice how that feels afterward.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 9, 'Tell a parent one specific thing you appreciate about how they raised you, even if it''s small.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 10, 'Decide how long your next family visit will be before you go, and mention the end time when you arrive.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 11, 'Look up a hotel, rental or friend''s spare room near your family''s home for your next trip, so you have a quiet place to return to.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 12, 'Step outside for a ten-minute walk around the block when a family conversation starts to heat up.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 13, 'Ask an aunt or uncle what your parent was like as a kid.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 14, 'Pull out one old family photo, and send a picture of it to the relative who is in it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 15, 'Ask a relative to teach you one family recipe step by step, and write it down in their words.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 16, 'Suggest a shared activity for your next visit, like a puzzle, a card game or a walk, so there is something to do besides talk.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 17, 'Practice this line out loud: "I''m not going to talk about my job search, but I''d love to hear about your garden."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 18, 'Send a short, honest note after a family argument: "I didn''t like how that went. I''d like to try again."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 19, 'Plan a simple outing with one sibling, just the two of you, like coffee, a movie or a hike.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 20, 'Bring a small gift to your next family visit, such as flowers, a dessert or something for the host''s pantry.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 21, 'Ask your mother-in-law or father-in-law about a hobby they love, and let them talk as long as they like.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 22, 'Call an aging parent and ask how their week went before you bring up any appointments or chores.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 23, 'Text your adult child something with no advice in it, just "Thinking of you, hope your day is good."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 24, 'Learn one thing your stepchild or stepparent enjoys, and ask them about it next time you see them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 25, 'Write the names of two or three people who feel like family to you, even if you are not related.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 26, 'Mark the birthdays of your siblings, nieces and nephews on your calendar for the year ahead.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 27, 'Offer to bring a side dish to the next summer cookout, and ask the host what would help most.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 28, 'Mute a family group chat for the evening if it''s buzzing, and catch up in the morning with fresh eyes.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 29, 'Decide ahead of time whether you can lend money to a relative this season, and practice a kind no if you can''t.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 30, 'When a relative shares a political view you disagree with, try "I see it differently, but I''m glad we can still have dinner together."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 31, 'Think back to the last time you snapped at a relative and notice what came first: tired, hungry or rushed?'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 32, 'Eat a real snack before you walk into a family gathering, so hunger isn''t running the conversation.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 33, 'Pick a seat at the next family meal next to someone who''s easy to be with.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 34, 'As a passenger on a family road trip, offer to be in charge of the music or the snacks.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 35, 'Offer to wash dishes at your parents'' home after dinner, and use it as a quiet minute.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 36, 'Ask a cousin what they''ve been up to lately, and listen for one detail to ask about later.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 37, 'Write down one thing a relative does that bothers you, and one reason they might do it that isn''t about you.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 38, 'Before a video call with family, put a glass of water and a notepad nearby so you can stay settled.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 39, 'Say "Let''s talk about something happier" with a smile when the conversation turns to gossip about another relative.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 40, 'Ask your parent about the hardest job they ever had and what got them through it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 41, 'Reach out to a relative who might be lonely this season with a short call or card.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 42, 'It''s okay to keep your distance from a relative who is unkind to you; spend some time today with a friend who feels like home.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 43, 'Prepare a quick exit line for when you need to leave: "I''ve loved this, and I need to head out now."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 44, 'Agree with your partner on a time to leave the in-laws'' house before you get in the car.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 45, 'Thank the person who cooked at the last family meal you went to, with a text or a call.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 46, 'Send your grandparent a photo of something you made, cooked or grew.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 47, 'Name one thing your sibling is good at and tell them so.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 48, 'When a relative gives unwanted advice, try "Thanks, I''ll think about that," and move on.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 49, 'Choose one topic you will gently steer away from at your next visit, and have a new topic ready.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 50, 'Ask your grown kid whether they''d like help with something before you offer it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 51, 'Lay out a family photo album on the coffee table at your next gathering and see who picks it up.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 52, 'Notice how you speak to your family compared to how you speak to friends, and pick one kindness to carry over.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 53, 'List what you need to feel okay during a visit, like a morning walk, quiet time or a phone call with a friend.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 54, 'Take a short break in the bathroom or on the porch at a family event if you need a reset; nobody needs a reason.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 55, 'Text a sibling a memory of a family pet you both loved.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 56, 'Ask your parent if there''s a family story they wish more people knew.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 57, 'Make a playlist of songs your family loved when you were growing up, and share it in the group chat.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 58, 'Leave a family dinner while it''s still pleasant instead of staying until everyone is tired.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 59, 'Call a relative while you take a walk in the park, so the movement keeps you calm.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 60, 'Offer to help an aging parent with one small task, like setting up a phone or changing a lightbulb.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 61, 'Set a reminder to call your grandparent on a regular evening that suits you both.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 62, 'If a sibling brings up an old hurt, try saying "I hear that it still matters to you" before you explain your side.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 63, 'Put together a back-to-school care package for a niece, nephew or grandchild, with a note wishing them a good start.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 64, 'Rake leaves or help with a fall yard chore at a parent''s home, and chat while you work.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 65, 'Ask a step-sibling or half-sibling about a tradition from their side of the family.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 66, 'Accept one small invitation from a relative this month that you''d normally skip, if it feels safe and easy.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 67, 'Before a family reunion, look over the guest list and choose two relatives you''d like to catch up with.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 68, 'Count slowly to five before answering a question that feels like a jab.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 69, 'Write a thank-you note to a relative who helped you when you were younger.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 70, 'When your in-law does something kind, mention it to your partner so they hear it too.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 71, 'Ask your mom what music she listened to as a teenager, and play one song together.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 72, 'Change the subject kindly with a question: "Speaking of that, how''s your trip planning going?"'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 73, 'Picture your next family visit going well, and write down one thing you''ll do to help it go that way.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 74, 'Choose a winter tradition that fits everyone, like a walk to see the lights or a movie night, rather than one tied to a single faith.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 75, 'Ask the host of a holiday gathering what time they''d like guests to head home, so you can plan around it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 76, 'Keep your coat and keys in one spot at a family event so leaving is easy when you''re ready.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 77, 'Have one sentence ready if someone asks about your love life: "Nothing to report, but I''ve been loving my new hobby."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 78, 'Set the table or carry dishes at a family meal, so you have something to do with your hands.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 79, 'Pack a deck of cards for the next family gathering, and invite the kids and grandparents to play.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 80, 'Ask your grandmother which dish she always made for special occasions and why.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 81, 'Cook a family recipe at home tonight, and send a photo to the relative who made it best.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 82, 'When a cousin posts good news, reply with a real sentence, not just a heart.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 83, 'Decide before a visit which chores you''ll help with and which you''ll leave to the hosts.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 84, 'Write down one money limit with family, like how much you can lend or give this year, and keep it somewhere private.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 85, 'Practice a warm no to a money request: "I can''t lend money right now, but I''m glad you told me what''s going on."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 86, 'Say yes to a favor for a relative only after you check your calendar, not on the spot.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 87, 'Tell your in-laws one thing you enjoy about visiting them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 88, 'Learn the name of your in-law''s best friend or pet, and ask about them next time.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 89, 'Ask your partner what their family''s unspoken rules are, like when to arrive or who sits where.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 90, 'Share one of your family''s quirks with your partner with a laugh, so you both feel like you''re on the same team.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 91, 'Sit with an aging parent while they look through old letters or papers, and let them tell you about them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 92, 'Ask your father what he''d like to keep doing for himself as he gets older, and listen to the answer.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 93, 'Drive an aging parent to an appointment and stay for lunch afterward, if you have the time.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 94, 'Let your adult child make a decision you wouldn''t make, and say "I trust you to figure it out."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 95, 'Ask your adult kid what kind of visit would feel best for them: short, long, at home or out.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 96, 'Invite your adult child to choose the restaurant next time you meet.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 97, 'Write down how often you''d honestly like to see each close relative, and compare it with how often you do.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 98, 'Text a stepparent on their birthday with a warm message, even a short one.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 99, 'Make room for your stepchild''s other family by asking how their weekend there went, without judgment.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 100, 'In a blended family, plan one small activity that''s new to everyone, so no one is the expert.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 101, 'Invite chosen family to a meal and call it what it is: family dinner.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 102, 'Write a note to a friend who has been like a sibling to you, telling them what that means.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 103, 'Lean on a friend after a hard family call: text them "Rough chat with family, can I vent for five minutes?"'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 104, 'Plan a small celebration for a relative''s milestone birthday, like a card everyone signs.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 105, 'At a family reunion, ask an older relative to point out who is who in an old group photo.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 106, 'Sketch a simple family tree to bring to a big reunion so younger relatives can place everyone.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 107, 'Before a summer cookout, decide how long you''ll stay, and plan something pleasant to do afterward.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 108, 'Bring a lawn game to a family picnic, like a frisbee or horseshoes, so people can play instead of debate.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 109, 'Walk with a relative after a big meal instead of staying at the table.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 110, 'Ask a teenage niece or nephew to show you a song or video they like, and enjoy it with them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 111, 'Put your phone in your bag during a family dinner and catch up on messages later.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 112, 'Notice the first sign that you''re getting irritated with family, like a clenched jaw, and soften it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 113, 'Write down three topics that reliably go well with your family, and keep the list on your phone.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 114, 'When a parent asks a question that feels like criticism, answer only the question and skip the defense.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 115, 'Try "You might be right" to end a small disagreement with a relative that isn''t worth the energy.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 116, 'Post a family group chat message that asks for nothing: a photo of your morning coffee or the sunset.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 117, 'If the family group chat gets tense, share something neutral, like a recipe or a pet picture, to lighten the mood.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 118, 'Reply to a heated group chat message privately instead of in front of everyone.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 119, 'Leave a family group chat that drains you, or ask to be added only to the planning thread.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 120, 'Ask your parent what they wish they''d known about raising kids, simply out of curiosity.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 121, 'Ask a grandparent how they celebrated holidays when they were a child.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 122, 'Record a short voice memo of a relative telling a favorite family story, with their permission.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 123, 'Scan or photograph three old family photos and share them with a cousin who might not have seen them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 124, 'Label the back of one old photo with names and a year before the details are forgotten.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 125, 'Bake something from a family recipe card and bring a few to the relative who wrote it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 126, 'Ask your sibling which family tradition they''d like to keep and which they''d happily drop.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 127, 'Set up a sibling video call with no agenda, just to catch up.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 128, 'Tell a sibling you''re sorry for something small from long ago, if it still sits with you.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 129, 'Ask your sister or brother how they''re really doing, and wait through the pause.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 130, 'Agree with a sibling on how to split a family task, like calling a grandparent or planning a gift.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 131, 'Suggest taking turns hosting a family event, so it doesn''t always fall to the same person.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 132, 'Be a good guest: make your bed, tidy your things and thank your hosts before you leave.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 133, 'Show visiting family where the snacks and towels are, so they can help themselves.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 134, 'Give visiting relatives a little time to themselves each day, like an hour to rest or explore.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 135, 'Plan one meal out during a long family visit, so no one has to cook and the setting changes.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 136, 'Pick a quiet restaurant for a family meal, so everyone can hear each other.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 137, 'Run a solo errand during a long visit to give yourself some breathing room.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 138, 'Ask a relative to join you on an errand, since side-by-side tasks can be easier than face-to-face talks.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 139, 'Before you jump in to fix a family member''s problem, ask "Do you want ideas or just someone to listen?"'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 140, 'Respect a relative''s faith or lack of it by asking what a tradition means to them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 141, 'When a relative brings up politics, ask "What''s on your mind about it?" and listen without arguing back.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 142, 'Decide on one belief topic you won''t debate this year, and choose a kind phrase to set it aside.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 143, 'Say "We see this differently, and I still love you," and then move to another topic.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 144, 'Remind yourself before a visit that you can''t change a relative, only how you respond.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 145, 'Write a sticky note for your mirror before a family event: "I can stay calm, and I can leave."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 146, 'Look back over this challenge so far and pick one small step that helped with family, then do it again today.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 147, 'After a tense family moment, wait until you''re calm, then send a message that starts with "I''ve been thinking about our talk."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 148, 'Name your part in a recent family argument to yourself, even if it''s small, without being hard on yourself.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 149, 'Offer a fresh start to a relative after a spat: "Can we try that conversation again over coffee?"'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 150, 'Accept a relative''s clumsy attempt to make up, even if it isn''t the apology you hoped for, if that feels right to you.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 151, 'Ask for a short pause in a heated family conversation: "Let''s take a break and pick this up after lunch."'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 152, 'Go back to a relative after a break and start with one thing you agree on.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 153, 'In the morning before a family event, spend five quiet minutes with coffee or tea on your own.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 154, 'Plan something restful for the evening after a family visit, like a bath, a show or an early night.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 155, 'On a video call with family, move to a room with good light and a door you can close.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 156, 'Keep a family video call to a set length, like twenty minutes, and say so at the start.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 157, 'Wave a grandchild into the video call and let them give you a tour of their room.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 158, 'Invite a relative to meet at a park for a walk instead of a long sit-down visit.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 159, 'Bring a thermos of hot cocoa to a fall walk with a parent or sibling.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 160, 'Visit a farmers market or fall festival with a relative, and pick out something to cook together.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 161, 'Pick apples or pumpkins with family members of different ages, so everyone has a job.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 162, 'Plan a holiday gathering that leaves room for every tradition at the table, or none, and say all are welcome.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 163, 'Ask relatives which winter foods mean the most to them, and include one from each household.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 164, 'Light candles or string lights with family on a dark winter evening, for whatever reason feels right to you.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 165, 'Send winter greetings to relatives who celebrate differently from you, using their words for the season.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 166, 'Keep spring cleaning at a parent''s home short and focused: one closet, one shelf or one box.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 167, 'Ask your parent the story behind one object in their home before you help sort it.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 168, 'Ask an aging parent what they''d like help with, rather than deciding for them.'),
  ('getting-along-better-with-family', 169, 'Start a shared note with your siblings about a parent''s needs, so no one carries it all in their head.'),
